00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:36.650
Hi.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:39.020
My name is Mae White Wesson.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.860
I was a member of the
MIT class of 1975.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.890
And I graduated with
a degree in Course 7.

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:49.880
I was a member of the MIT
Gospel Choir from its inception

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.340
until my graduation.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.990
I joined the choir
because I love to sing.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.010
I had always been a
member of whatever choir

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:02.270
was age appropriate for
me from my childhood

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:03.890
until my graduation.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:08.210
I enjoyed being in the
choir, sharing time

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.060
with other Black
students at MIT.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:14.450
Because at that time,
there were not many of us.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:19.490
My class had 25 or
47 Black students

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:22.820
in a class of about 1,000.

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.170
So it was quite a culture shock.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:28.520
I had not gone to
an integrated school

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:33.230
before my appearance
in Cambridge.

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:37.970
So participating in the choir
gave me a sense of continuity.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:42.920
It gave me a system of support.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:48.440
It gave me time
spent in something

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:50.522
that I was familiar with.

00:07:50.522 --> 00:07:51.980
I think the importance
of the choir

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:55.670
was that it showed that
MIT was changing a bit.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.380
There was some
attempt at diversity,

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.230
at least as far as adding
Black students to the ranks.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:05.990
I think we made a big
impression on the campus

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:09.470
and also in the greater
Boston/Cambridge community.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:11.450
We had concerts.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:13.160
We had programs.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:16.430
We sang in the Great Hall.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:21.560
It was a joyous
time and experience.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:24.290
Hello, this is Michael Dixon.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:28.760
I was at MIT from 1984 to 1988.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:32.120
And I was a bass in the Gospel
Choir, sometimes a tenor,

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.100
and they forced me to.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.110
I just wanted to
explain why the Gospel

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:41.210
Choir was so near and dear to my
heart during that time in MIT.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.820
It was also one of the
best support activities

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.640
that I was able to be a part
of and I had a privilege

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.385
to participate in.

00:08:48.385 --> 00:08:49.760
There are a lot
of great friends,

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.400
a lot of great challenges
that we face together.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.910
And those things have
impacted me to this day.

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:59.990
As far as memories go, I want
to just remember the times

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.860
that we sang in Kresge with
the New Jersey Mass Choir.

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:08.300
There was an awesome poster
along with that concert.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.370
That was the one that
was very colorful.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.810
It was just a blessing to
be a part of that piece.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.540
I remember the
times that we were

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:21.890
able to connect with people
like Philips Bryant, who

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:22.775
sang Going Up Yonder.

00:09:22.775 --> 00:09:26.270
It's one of those songs we
always used to love to sing.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:27.950
She was our Aretha Franklin.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:31.140
She was a powerful voice.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:35.370
I also remember singing
some original song

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:40.040
from Daryl Gaskin, Daryl
"The Roof Is On Fire" Gaskin.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:43.490
I remember singing Psalms
150, I'm Saved by Grace.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:45.950
One of my favorites
was 2,000 Years Ago.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:46.940
Awesome.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:50.540
We sang some more original
songs from Tyrone Williams.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:56.180
Remember having Easter service
at Kresge with St. Paul's

00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:00.590
A.M.E. And I remember being
able to go into the organ

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:03.410
and hear it play at Kresge.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:05.580
I remember doing a tour.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.660
We went to tour the East Coast.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:10.520
It was a blessing.

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:14.690
But one of the years we did
tour was during the Challenger

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:17.270
explosion, where
we lost Ron McNair

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:18.860
and a few other astronauts.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:22.460
Ron McNair was an MIT physics
major and grad school.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:24.470
And he was known by
many of the people.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.400
We were actually going to visit.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.030
So it was a really solemn time.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:33.980
We also sang Let
Mount Zion Rejoice.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:36.200
And we dedicated that
song during the tour.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.720
But also, we sang
it, the dedication

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:43.230
of the building, Ron
McNair building as well.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.850
I remember connecting with
people such as my wife, Crystal

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:50.660
Dixon, who it was a
pleasure to work with

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:53.532
and to be able to interact
with and so many other people.

00:10:53.532 --> 00:10:54.740
It was just an awesome thing.

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.420
But some people stick out.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:01.970
For example, China Altman, who
was just a courageous woman who

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:06.170
was part of the media at MIT.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:08.810
And she's the one who helped
us get those posters done

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:10.250
amongst other things.

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:11.990
But she was just a blessing.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:16.520
And just to see how she was a
benefit to us, and we, I think,

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:17.990
impacted her as well.

